items.statement:
At an ordinary point x ∈ (𝔈_{p,2})^{≥1}, exactly one of the following holds for p_1^*H → p_2^*H:
1. both are multiplicative and the map is an isomorphism; then p_2^*I^{−1} → p_1^*I^{−1} is an isomorphism at x;
2. both are étale and the map is an isomorphism; then p_2^*I^{−1} → p_1^*I^{−1} is an isomorphism at x;
3. p_1^*H is étale, p_2^*H is multiplicative and the map is zero at x; then p_2^*I^{−1} → p_1^*I^{−1} factors through p·p_1^*I^{−1} over the local ring at x.

routes.reason:
The route's 271 items are Pilloni's p-adic families of coherent cohomology in degrees 0 and 1 for the Siegel threefold (§§1, 7–15). They comprise:
- the integral T-operator and the Klingen U-operator;
- the deep Klingen tower and the Λ-adic sheaves over the p-rank ≥ 1 locus;
- higher Hida control and perfectness over Λ;
- overconvergent Klingen-level cohomology with compact U, and small-slope classicality;
- the comparison M† → M, and Theorems 1.1–1.2.

No atlas layer plans these. PadicFamilies owns ordinary projectors, control theorems and eigenvarieties, but only for modular curves and totally real fields, in one degree (L0–L5). OverconvergentAutomorphicForms O8 supplies Siegel coefficient sheaves, and O7 says it does not claim Hida's ordinary-projector or control theorems. So PROTOCOL 15 makes this a Part II of PadicFamilies, starting where it stops. Degree-0 Siegel Hida theory is its first layer. A source route cannot hold these items, because no existing layer covers them.

It stays one roadmap because Theorem 1.1's proof joins higher Hida theory (§§7–11) and higher Coleman theory (§§12–14) through M† and §14.8, with a shared Klingen U and a shared p-rank ≥ 1 locus.

General material is imported from its owners:
- projectors and spectral theory: L0a, L2a, L3, L4;
- Hasse invariants, spherical operators and p-adic Siegel forms: SiegelModularFormsModPn;
- coherent cohomology, the characteristic-0 Hecke actions and the automorphic description: IntegralCoherentHeckeComplexes;
- models and compactifications: ShimuraVarietiesHondaTatePartII and ShimuraCompactificationsPartIIHodgeTypeIntegralBoundary. Also the atlas stages that plan the planned items this roadmap uses: PELModuli, ShimuraData, ShimuraCompactifications, AdicSpacesPartII, AutomorphicBundles, KTheoryLowDegrees, and the Tau Ceti completed group algebra.

It sits upstream of GSp4NonregularModularityLifting and AbelianSurfacesPotentialModularity, and never imports GSp4NonregularModularityLifting.

Pilloni's §§6.3–6.4, together with the spherical parts of §§8.2, 11.2, 13.3.1, 14.8 and 14.9, build genus-two Siegel objects modulo p^n. These are:
- the second Hasse invariant Ha′;
- the p-rank strata of X_n and X_par,n and their local structure;
- canonical lifts of powers of the Hasse invariant, and the spherical two-affine covering of the p-rank ≥ 1 locus;
- Hartogs extension across the p-rank-0 locus;
- the integral spherical operator T_{p,2} over the p-rank ≥ 1 locus;
- p-adic Siegel forms on the ordinary Igusa tower and their q-expansions.

The proposed roadmap SiegelModularFormsModPn of CG20 already plans these objects: h̃ on its non-ordinary locus S, the strata, Koblitz's section, the lifts, Hartogs, operators at p that are finite over the p-rank ≥ 1 locus, and q-expansions. PROTOCOL 15 gives each piece one owner, so this route joins that proposal, with the same id, parent and title, and does not open a second roadmap. A source route is impossible, because the roadmap is not yet in the atlas.

The other candidates do not fit:
- HodgeTateAndCanonicalSubgroups T0 owns Ha of a general BT₁, which goes there by source route. It has no genus-two strata.
- The integral-model Part IIs, ShimuraVarietiesHondaTatePartII and ShimuraCompactificationsPartIIHodgeTypeIntegralBoundary, supply Y_par, the Iwahori-level moduli, X_par and C_{p,2}. They must stay upstream, so they cannot own Hasse-invariant geometry.
- HigherHidaAndColemanTheory is a consumer and keeps the Klingen-level constructions, including degree-0 Siegel Hida theory.
- No atlas layer builds the Siegel ordinary Igusa tower. HodgeTateAndCanonicalSubgroups T0–T5 serve the Hilbert route of BHW, PadicFamilies L0 builds p-adic forms for modular curves, and HigherHidaAndColemanTheory's IG(p^m) lives over the Klingen p-rank ≥ 1 tower.

This follows the route critic's split, with one narrowing. The spherical correspondences over the p-rank ≥ 1 locus belong here. Only T_{p,2} has a sheaf-level integrality proof (Lemma 14.9.1), so the T_{p,1}-type operators (CG20's T₂ and Q₂) are planned on H⁰ only; neither source proves more. The Klingen U, its analytic versions and T = T₁∘T₂ belong in HigherHidaAndColemanTheory. The characteristic-0 actions belong in IntegralCoherentHeckeComplexes.

sourceIssues.correction:
The correction that fits the rest of the paper is to take the positive system of the lower-triangular Borel, which is the negative of HAL v3's system. The changes in §5.1.1 are:
- positive roots {e2−e1, −2e1+e3, −e1−e2+e3, −2e2+e3};
- α1 = e2 − e1 (the compact root);
- α2 = −2e2 + e3 (unchanged);
- α1^∨ = f2 − f1 and α2^∨ = −f2;
- ρ = (−2, −1; 0).
The matrix of i stays as printed: i(α1) = −f2 = α2^∨ and i(α2) = f2 − f1 = α1^∨, and the matrix is symmetric and unimodular. The coset X*(T) + (−1,−2;0) in §5.1.6 needs no change, because (−1,−2;0) − (−2,−1;0) = (1,−1;0) lies in X*(T).
An alternative correction keeps the printed list and ρ and changes α2 to −2e1+e3, α2^∨ to −f1 and the matrix. That makes §5.1.1 consistent on its own, but it leaves §5.1.6 and §15.2.1 wrong. For holomorphic λ (λ1 < λ2 ≤ 0), λ is not in the closure of the printed dominant chamber. So that fix is not the right one for the paper as a whole, and it changes the matrix for no reason.

sourceIssues.reason:
I redid the computation from the moduli problem.

**Setup.** C_Y(p^m) (§10.1, p. 57) parametrises triples (G, H_m, L) where:
- L ⊂ G[p^2] is totally isotropic;
- L[p] has rank p^3 and L ∩ H_m = 0;
- L has rank p^4 (footnote 16).

At an ordinary point, write G[p^∞] = T^∨⊗μ_{p^∞} ⊕ T⊗Q_p/Z_p, with dual bases f_i and e_i. Here L^m = ℓ⊗μ_p for a line ℓ, and isotropy forces L^et = {t ∈ T/p^2 : ⟨ℓ,t⟩ ≡ 0 mod p}. In a basis with ℓ = f_2 this gives L^et = ⟨e_1/p^2, e_2/p⟩. Lemma 10.1.1 (p. 57) confirms this: L∩H_1^⊥ is (p,p) and the remaining part is Z/p^2.

**The vertical maps.** On Tate modules, T → T′ = Z_p e_1/p^2 ⊕ Z_p e_2/p, so A = diag(p^2, p). On multiplicative parts, M → M/(f_2⊗μ_p), so B = diag(1, p). These are exactly the matrices printed on p. 61. Since p^2λ = f^∨λ′f, the bases e_i′ and f_i′ are dual for the principal polarisation of G′ = G/L. So Q′ is symmetric, as printed.

**The compatibility condition.** For the extension model G = (T^∨⊗Ĝ_m ⊕ T⊗Q_p)/graph(−q), a morphism exists only if q′∘A = B∘q. Entrywise this reads Q′_ij^{a_j} = Q_ij^{b_i}. The transposed convention, which is the one that makes the p. 39 diagram consistent, gives the same result for the diagonal entries and for a symmetric Q′:
- entry (1,1): (1+X′)^{p^2} = 1+X;
- entries (1,2) and (2,1): (1+Z′)^p = 1+Z;
- entry (2,2): Y′ = Y.

**Direct count of lifts.** I also counted the lifts of L directly:
- the lift x_1 = (m_1, e_1/p^2) needs a p^2-th root of Q_11 (p^2 choices);
- the f_1-component of x_2 = (m_2, e_2/p) needs a p-th root of Q_12 (p choices);
- isotropy then fixes the f_2-component of m_1;
- the condition that L[p] has rank p^3 forces px_2 = 0, so Y′ = Y.

**No relabelling saves the printed ring.** Whatever the ordering, the off-diagonal parameter gets exponent p and the diagonal entries get p^2 and 1. So no relabelling gives Z′ the exponent p^2. The printed ring also contradicts the diagram printed right under it, whose (1,1) entries force the exponent p^2 on X′. The text layer agrees with the image, so this is not a pdftotext artefact.

**HAL v3.** HAL v3 (PDF p. 51, printed p. 50) has the same error. There it is written with "=" signs: "((1 + X′)^p − 1 = X, (1 + Z′)^{p^2} − 1 = Z, Y′ = Y)". It has the same diagram: (p^2,0;0,p), (1,0;0,p), (X′,Z′;Z′,Y′).

**Why nothing else changes.** Write u = 1+X′ and v = 1+Z′. For R[u,v]/(u^a − (1+X), v^b − (1+Z)), the basis u^i v^j has trace 0 unless i = j = 0. So Tr(R′) = ab·R with ab = p^3 in both versions, and dividing by p^3 still preserves integrality. The lemma and everything built on it stand.

